        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 229,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8823/1 Desmoplastic fibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 397,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 230,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60346,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 230,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F7B &amp; XH6YK5 Neoplasms of uncertain behaviour of bone and articular cartilage &amp; Desmoplastic fibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 398,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 231,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60347,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 231,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> desmoid tumour of bone.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 399,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 232,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60348,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60349,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Desmoplastic fibroma may involve any bone but is most frequently found in the mandible, followed by long bones (femur, radius, tibia) and pelvic bones.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60350,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "<h5><em>Signs and symptoms</em></h5>\r\n<p>Some tumours are found incidentally in asymptomatic patients. However, most patients have a longstanding history of pain or bone deformity. In about 10% of cases, a pathological fracture is the presenting symptom.</p>\r\n<h5><em>Imaging</em></h5>\r\n<p>Radiographically, desmoplastic fibroma is a well-defined, lobulated, radiolucent lesion that may expand the host bone. Intralesional trabeculation is often present. Larger lesions may destroy cortical bone and extend into surrounding soft tissue, which is well appreciated by MRI. Desmoplastic fibroma has low signal intensity in both T1- and T2-weighted MRI images and may show increased uptake with bone scintigraphy or FDG&nbsp;PET.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-29T16:15:22"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60351,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Desmoplastic fibroma accounts for &lt;&nbsp;0.1% of all primary bone tumours. The tumour mainly affects adolescents and young adults, with near-equal sex distribution.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60352,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60353,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60354,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Due to its high collagen content, desmoplastic fibroma is a firm, creamy-white tumour with a whorled architecture. The tumour border is well defined and has scalloped margins, even when it extends into soft tissue.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60355,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Desmoplastic fibroma has an infiltrative growth pattern and typically consists of a tumour of low cellularity composed of fascicles of bland nondescript spindle cells set in a collagenous matrix. Mitoses are scant. Necrosis is not present. The fibroblastic spindle cells show variable immunoreactivity for SMA; &beta;-catenin can be present {30159138}, most commonly in the cytoplasm {16153468}. The differential diagnosis includes fibrous dysplasia, low-grade central osteosarcoma, low-grade myofibroblastic sarcoma, myoepithelial tumours, follicular dendritic cell tumours, and synovial sarcoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60356,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60357,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "<p>In view of recent advances in molecular pathology allowing classification of bone tumours more objectively, the diagnosis of desmoplastic fibroma should be one of exclusion. In particular, fibrous dysplasia and low-grade central osteosarcoma should be excluded by molecular tests for <em>GNAS</em> single-nucleotide variants and <em>MDM2</em> amplification, respectively. There are reports of two cases of desmoplastic fibroma in bone revealing a <em>CTNNB1</em> mutation: one p.Thr41Ala {24206198} and one p.Ser45Phe alteration {30159138}. Both cases exhibited nuclear &beta;-catenin immunostaining. Previously, <em>CTNNB1</em> mutations were not detected in a series of 13&nbsp;cases {16153468}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-06T16:05:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60358,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> a bland spindle cell lesion in bone; collagenous matrix.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> exclude fibrous dysplasia by showing the absence of <em>GNAS</em> mutations; exclude low-grade central osteosarcoma by showing the absence of <em>MDM2</em> amplification.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-06T16:05:50"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60359,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-11-26T16:12:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 60360,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 168,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "<p>Prognosis and prediction are difficult to predict because so few cases have been reported. Desmoplastic fibroma is a slowly progressive and locally aggressive tumour, which may recur after curettage or intralesional excision {23459513}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-01-06T16:06:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]